{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a78c2e07f5bac729", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\t \n2\t\n3\t Discriminative Axis, Not Data Volume: What a Contrastive Corpus Teaches an Audio Embedding \n4\t\n5\t Report GitHub Issue \n6\t × \n7\t\n8\t Title: \n9\t\n10\t Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: \n11\t\n12\t Description: \n13\t\n14\t Submit without GitHub \n15\t Submit in GitHub \n16\t\n17\t arXiv is now an independent nonprofit! \n18\t Learn more \n19\t &times; \n20\t\n21\t Back to arXiv \n22\t\n23\t Why HTML? \n24\t\n25\t Report Issue \n26\t\n27\t Back to Abstract \n28\t\n29\t Download PDF \n30\t\n31\t Abstract \n32\t 1 Introduction \n33\t\n34\t 2 Related work \n35\t\n36\t Shortcut learning and feature suppression. \n37\t Hard negatives and batch composition. \n38\t Audio–text contrastive embeddings. \n39\t\n40\t 3 Setup \n41\t\n42\t Model. \n43\t Pretraining corpus. \n44\t Fine-tuning. \n45\t Evaluation. \n46\t\n47\t 4 Lexical speech trades emotion for keywords \n48\t 5 The deficit is not capacity \n49\t 6 The deficit is not data volume \n50\t 7 The deficit is not truncation \n51\t 8 The intervention is otherwise inert \n52\t 9 Caption separability predicts what is learned \n53\t\n54\t 10 Mechanism \n55\t\n56\t The negative set is not the batch. \n57\t A testable prediction. \n58\t\n59\t 11 Testing the mechanism \n60\t\n61\t 11.1 Similarity is not enough \n62\t\n63\t 11.2 Collision recovers emotion \n64\t\n65\t A second, non-acted benchmark. \n66\t\n67\t 12 Discussion \n68\t\n69\t 13 Limitations \n70\t\n71\t Data contamination. \n72\t\n73\t 14 Conclusion \n74\t References \n75\t\n76\t License: CC BY 4.0\n77\t\n78\tarXiv:2608.01560v1 [cs.CL] 03 Aug 2026 \n79\t\n80\t Discriminative Axis, Not Data Volume: What a Contrastive\n81\t Corpus Teaches an Audio Embedding \n82\t\n83\t Abdul Basit Tonmoy \n84\t Eximius Labs  Wabash College \n85\t atonmoy27@wabash.edu \n86\t\n87\t Abstract \n88\t Scaling the corpus is the default remedy when a contrastive\n89\trepresentation lacks an attribute. We report a case where it does\n90\tnothing, and identify what does: adding a lexical-speech round to a\n91\tfrozen-base multimodal embedding model raises zero-shot keyword spotting\n92\tby 76 points while reducing speech-emotion recognition by 14.\n93\tThe loss is not a capacity limit: fine-tuning on 7,442 clips from a\n94\tprosody-controlled corpus recovers emotion past its pre-speech level at\n95\ta five-point keyword cost. Nor is it data volume: 29,428 mined clips\n96\twhose captions explicitly name emotions, at matched exposure, move\n97\temotion by − 0.0007 -0.0007 . The difference is structural: a contrastive\n98\tobjective encodes an attribute only when the in-batch negatives cannot\n99\tbe separated without it; the controlled corpus holds sentence content\n100\tfixed, so prosody is the only separating signal, whereas mined captions\n101\tname emotion yet remain separable by scene content. Intervention on the\n102\tsame audio confirms causality: raising caption similarity does not\n103\trecover emotion, but collapsing caption diversity so that emotion\n104\tbecomes the only separating axis recovers it by 8.9 points across three\n105\tseeds, with a smaller, same-signed gain on a non-acted corpus, while\n106\tkeyword accuracy trades back. Corpus structure, not size or caption\n107\tvocabulary, controls what a contrastive audio embedding encodes. \n108\t\n109\t Discriminative Axis, Not Data Volume: What a Contrastive\n110\t Corpus Teaches an Audio Embedding \n111\t\n112\t Abdul Basit Tonmoy \n113\t\n114\t Eximius Labs  Wabash College \n115\t\n116\t atonmoy27@wabash.edu \n117\t\n118\t Figure 1: Corpus structure, not data volume, decides what a contrastive\n119\taudio embedding encodes. Adding a lexical-speech round to a frozen-base\n120\tmultimodal embedding model raises zero-shot keyword spotting by 76\n121\tpoints ( 0.133 → 0.894 0.133\\to 0.894 ) while reducing emotion recognition by 14\n122\t( 0.348 → 0.211 0.348\\to 0.211 , the grey baseline shown here). Arrows run from that\n123\tbaseline to each fine-tuned arm: 7,442 prosody-controlled clips\n124\t( Crema-D ) recover emotion past its pre-speech level (dashed) at a cost\n125\tof five keyword points, while a mined corpus four times larger, whose\n126\tcaptions all name emotion explicitly, moves emotion by − 0.0007 -0.0007 at\n127\tmatched exposure. Both axes are zero-shot accuracy. \n128\t\n129\t 1 Introduction \n130\t\n131\t When a learned representation underperforms on some attribute, the\n132\tstandard response is to add data containing that attribute. We report a\n133\tsetting where that response fails cleanly: extending the training corpus\n134\tof an audio embedding model with large quantities of lexical speech\n135\traised zero-shot keyword spotting by 76 points and reduced \n136\tspeech-emotion recognition by 14 (Figure  1 ). Both are\n137\tspeech tasks, evaluated in the same shared space, by the same protocol;\n138\tmore speech data made the model worse at one of them. \n139\t\n140\t Our setting is an audio embedding built by attaching a trained connector\n141\tand modality-gated adapters to a frozen vision-language embedding model  (Tonmoy et al., 2026 ) .\n142\tWe treat the regression as an opportunity rather than a defect, because\n143\tit permits a sharper question than “does more data help”: which \n144\tproperty of a contrastive corpus causes an attribute to be encoded? We\n145\teliminate three explanations in turn. The deficit is not capacity:\n146\tfine-tuning on 7,442 clips from a prosody-controlled corpus restores\n147\temotion past its pre-speech level (Section  5 ). It is not data\n148\tvolume: 29,428 mined clips whose captions explicitly name emotion, at\n149\tmatched exposure, move emotion by − 0.0007 -0.0007 (Section  6 ). It\n150\tis not representational truncation, and the mined fine-tune causes no\n151\tcollateral damage that could mask an effect (Sections  7 \n152\tand  8 ). \n153\t\n154\t What remains is that a contrastive objective is indifferent to\n155\tattributes it does not need. An attribute is learned when the in-batch\n156\tnegatives cannot be separated without it, and ignored when they can,\n157\teven if every caption in the corpus names it explicitly. Corpus\n158\tconstruction, not corpus size, decides this; a separability statistic\n159\tcomputable from the corpus alone predicts the outcome\n160\t(Section  9 ), and an intervention on the same audio confirms\n161\tthe account causally (Section  11 ). \n162\t\n163\t Our contributions are: \n164\t\n165\t • \n166\t\n167\t Large-scale lexical-speech supervision trades emotion\n168\trecognition for keyword recognition in a shared audio embedding,\n169\trather than improving speech understanding uniformly: + 76 +76 keyword\n170\tpoints, − 14 -14 emotion points (Table  1 ,\n171\tSection  4 ). \n172\t\n173\t • \n174\t\n175\t The loss is not capacity-bound: a corpus 120 × 120\\times smaller than\n176\tthe post-speech training corpus restores the lost attribute past its\n177\toriginal level, 0.211 → 0.508 0.211\\to 0.508 against a pre-speech 0.348 0.348 \n178\t(Table  2 , Section  5 ). \n179\t\n180\t • \n181\t\n182\t A matched-exposure negative control shows that four times the\n183\tdata, with captions that explicitly name the target attribute, moves\n184\temotion by − 0.0007 -0.0007 (Table  4 , Section  6 ). \n185\t\n186\t • \n187\t\n188\t Two further explanations fail: representational truncation\n189\trecovers only 18% of the gap (Table  5 ,\n190\tSection  7 ), and a nine-task board shows no general\n191\tdegradation that could mask an effect (Table  6 ,\n192\tSection  8 ). \n193\t\n194\t • \n195\t\n196\t A controlled test of the mechanism: restructuring the same corpus\n197\tso that emotion is the required discriminative axis recovers the\n198\tattribute by + 0.089 +0.089 across three seeds, whereas raising in-batch\n199\tcaption similarity alone does not (Tables  8 \n200\tand  9 , Figure  4 , Section  11 ). This\n201\tconverts the account from an observation across corpora into a\n202\tdemonstrated cause. \n203\t\n204\t 2 Related work \n205\t\n206\t Our finding sits at the intersection of three lines of work. \n207\t\n208\t Shortcut learning and feature suppression. \n209\t\n210\t A model trained\n211\twith a discriminative objective uses whichever feature minimizes the loss\n212\tmost cheaply, and need not represent alternatives\n213\t (Geirhos et al., 2020 ) . In contrastive learning this appears as\n214\t feature suppression : Chen et al. ( 2021 ) construct datasets\n215\twith explicitly competing features and show that a few bits of\n216\teasy-to-learn shared feature can suppress, and even fully prevent, the\n217\tlearning of competing ones. Robinson et al. ( 2021 ) show the same\n218\ttendency follows from optimizing the InfoNCE objective itself, and\n219\tpropose implicit feature modification to counter it. \n220\t\n221\t Both establish the phenomenon by manipulating the inputs \n222\t(synthetic competing features, or altered positives and negatives), and\n223\trecent work removes it algorithmically: Zhang et al. ( 2024 ) mitigate\n224\tsuppression with feature-aware negative sampling that draws an anchor’s\n225\tnegatives from its own cluster, and Bleeker et al. ( 2024 ) reduce\n226\tvision-language shortcuts with auxiliary reconstruction objectives. These\n227\ttreat suppression as a property of the sampler or loss to engineer away on\n228\ta fixed dataset. Our contribution is complementary and, to our knowledge,\n229\tnot made elsewhere: the same suppression is governed by the ordinary\n230\tconstruction of a real training corpus , at scale; it is not\n231\tremedied by adding data in which the suppressed attribute is explicitly\n232\tdescribed; and it is reversed by a structural change to the corpus rather\n233\tthan to the algorithm. We do not claim the phenomenon of suppression is\n234\tnew. We isolate the corpus as its operative variable with a\n235\tmatched-exposure control, and we do so in audio, where CLAP-family corpora\n236\tare usually discussed only in terms of scale and caption quality. \n237\t\n238\t Hard negatives and batch composition. \n239\t\n240\t A related line\n241\timproves contrastive representations by changing which negatives appear\n242\tin a batch, on the argument that easy negatives supply little gradient\n243\tsignal (Robinson et al., 2020 ) . That work treats batch composition as an\n244\toptimization device for a fixed target. We make a different claim: batch\n245\tcomposition determines which attribute is learned at all , not\n246\tmerely how efficiently a fixed objective converges. The prediction in\n247\tSection  10 connects the two, since it proposes recovering an\n248\tattribute purely by regrouping items already present in the corpus. \n249\t\n250\t Audio–text contrastive embeddings. \n251\t\n252\t Models in the CLAP family\n253\talign audio with free-form text and are evaluated zero-shot by embedding\n254\tcandidate class names (Elizalde et al., 2023 ; Wu et al., 2022 ) . Corpus\n255\tconstruction in this literature is discussed largely in terms of scale,\n256\tcaption quality, and noise filtering; Mei et al. ( 2023 ) , for\n257\tinstance, addresses caption quality at scale. Emotion in particular has\n258\tbeen built into CLAP-style models with explicit attribute supervision\n259\t (Pan et al., 2023 ) ; our question is orthogonal, namely when generic\n260\temotion-captioned data does and does not cause emotion to be encoded at\n261\tall. Probing studies of what a fixed CLAP embedding already contains\n262\trecover low-level acoustic attributes such as loudness and pitch\n263\t (Martel et al., 2026 ) ; we instead intervene on the corpus and study a\n264\thigher-level attribute whose encoding we show to be corpus-structure\n265\tdependent. To our knowledge the structural property we isolate, whether\n266\ta corpus makes an attribute necessary for discrimination, has\n267\tnot been separated from scale and caption content with a matched-exposure\n268\tcontrol. \n269\t\n270\t We do not claim that attribute competition is novel. The contribution is\n271\tthe controlled comparison that isolates corpus structure as the operative\n272\tvariable, in a setting where the intuitive remedy (more data, explicitly\n273\tcaptioned) demonstrably fails. \n274\t\n275\t 3 Setup \n276\t\n277\t Model. \n278\t\n279\t A 2B-parameter frozen vision–language embedding\n280\tmodel with a trained audio connector (a query-based resampler) and\n281\tmodality-gated deep adapters attached to each decoder layer. The gate is\n282\tclosed on non-audio inputs, so text, image, and video paths execute the\n283\tbase computation graph unchanged. All training in this paper updates only\n284\tthe connector and adapters; the base weights are never modified. The\n285\tembedding is Matryoshka-nested (Kusupati et al., 2022 ) with a\n286\tdefault read-out of 1024\n287\tdimensions. \n288\t\n289\t Pretraining corpus. \n290\t\n291\t 888,800 clips across eleven sources.\n292\tThe lexical-speech round introduced for this study contributes\n293\t ∼ \\sim 150K spoken-word clips, 70K read-speech clips, and a read-audiobook\n294\tset; the remaining sources are environmental audio and audio–caption\n295\tpairs. Pretraining uses a frozen-text memory bank, false-negative\n296\tmasking, and soft labels. \n297\t\n298\t Fine-tuning. \n299\t\n300\t All fine-tunes in Sections  5 \n301\tand  6 use an identical recipe, differing only in the corpus\n302\tand the step count: batch 128, no gradient accumulation, peak learning\n303\trate 3 × 10 − 5 3{\\times}10^{-5} , false-negative masking and soft labels disabled,\n304\tinitialized from the same post-speech checkpoint. Holding the recipe\n305\tfixed is what makes the two corpora comparable. \n306\t\n307\t Evaluation. \n308\t\n309\t We report two zero-shot classification tasks from\n310\tthe MAEB audio embedding benchmark in the mteb framework\n311\t (Muennighoff et al., 2022 ) : SpeechCommands \n312\t (Warden, 2018 ) (35-way keyword spotting)\n313\tand Ravdess (speech emotion). Both are scored by embedding the audio and\n314\tthe candidate class texts into the shared space and matching; neither is\n315\tfine-tuned on. Section  8 additionally reports a nine-task\n316\tboard spanning retrieval, classification, clustering, and reranking, to\n317\ttest for collateral effects. \n318\t\n319\t 4 Lexical speech trades emotion for keywords \n320\t\n321\t Adding the lexical-speech round raises keyword spotting by 76 points and\n322\tlowers emotion recognition by 14. Table  1 reports the two\n323\ttasks before and after the round is added to the pretraining corpus. \n324\t\n325\t SpeechCommands \n326\t Ravdess \n327\t\n328\t pre-speech \n329\t 0.133 \n330\t 0.348 \n331\t\n332\t post-speech \n333\t 0.894 \n334\t 0.211 \n335\t\n336\t Δ \\Delta \n337\t + 0.761 +0.761 \n338\t − 0.137 -0.137 \n339\t\n340\t Table 1: The lexical-speech round trades one speech attribute for\n341\tanother: keyword spotting improves by 76 points while emotion\n342\trecognition regresses by 14. Both tasks are zero-shot; bold marks the\n343\tbetter value in each column. \n344\t\n345\t The keyword gain is unsurprising: the spoken-word corpus pairs isolated\n346\twords with their transcriptions, which closely matches the form of the\n347\tevaluation. The emotion regression is the finding. Both tasks are speech;\n348\tan account in which “speech data improves speech understanding”\n349\tpredicts the wrong sign for one of them. \n350\t\n351\t Two explanations are available. Either the model lacks the capacity to\n352\trepresent both lexical content and prosody and the new supervision\n353\tconsumed it, or prosody was never required by the objective and the new\n354\tsupervision actively selected against it. The next two sections separate\n355\tthese. \n356\t\n357\t 5 The deficit is not capacity \n358\t\n359\t A corpus 120 × 120\\times smaller than the post-speech training corpus\n360\trestores the lost attribute past its pre-speech level, so the deficit is\n361\tnot a capacity limit. Crema-D (Cao et al., 2014 ) contains 7,442\n362\tclips in which 91 actors speak twelve fixed sentences across six\n363\temotions. Because lexical content is held approximately constant by\n364\tconstruction, two clips drawn into the same batch typically differ in\n365\tspeaker and emotion but not in what is said. \n366\t\n367\t We fine-tune the post-speech model on Crema-D for 150 and 400 steps\n368\t(Table  2 , Figure  2 ). \n369\t\n370\t Figure 2: Only the prosody-controlled corpus recovers emotion. Both\n371\tfine-tuning corpora are shown at two exposures each, against the\n372\tpost-speech baseline: Crema-D (7,442 clips) lifts emotion past its\n373\tpre-speech level of 0.348 (dashed) at a modest keyword cost, while the\n374\tmined corpus (29,428 clips, every caption naming emotion explicitly)\n375\tleaves emotion unchanged and raises keyword accuracy. Both metrics are\n376\tzero-shot accuracy and share one axis. \n377\t\n378\t fine-tune \n379\t clips \n380\t ep. \n381\t SpeechCommands \n382\t Ravdess \n383\t\n384\t none \n385\t – \n386\t – \n387\t 0.894 \n388\t 0.211 \n389\t\n390\t Crema-D \n391\t 7,442 \n392\t 2.6 \n393\t 0.845 \n394\t 0.508 \n395\t\n396\t Crema-D \n397\t 7,442 \n398\t 6.9 \n399\t 0.832 \n400\t 0.537 \n401\t\n402\t Table 2: A prosody-controlled corpus 120 × 120\\times smaller than the\n403\tpost-speech training corpus restores emotion past its pre-speech level\n404\tof 0.348, at a cost of five points of keyword accuracy. The Crema-D \n405\trows are 150- and 400-step fine-tunes of the post-speech model. \n406\t\n407\t Emotion recovers to 0.508 after 150 steps, exceeding the pre-speech value\n408\tof 0.348, while keyword accuracy falls only to 0.845, still more than\n409\tsix times the pre-speech keyword score. The exchange rate is roughly six\n410\temotion points per keyword point at 150 steps, degrading to about two\n411\tbetween 150 and 400, so the shorter schedule is the better operating\n412\tpoint. \n413\t\n414\t This rules out the capacity account. The model can represent prosody; a\n415\tcorpus two orders of magnitude smaller than the post-speech training\n416\tcorpus is sufficient to reinstate it. What the lexical-speech round did\n417\twas not exhaust capacity but fail to require prosody. \n418\t\n419\t 6 The deficit is not data volume \n420\t\n421\t Four times the data, with captions that explicitly name the target\n422\tattribute, moves emotion by − 0.0007 -0.0007 (Table  4 ); the deficit\n423\tis not data volume either. If prosody merely needed to be present \n424\tin supervision, then any corpus whose captions describe vocal emotion\n425\tshould serve, and more of it should serve better. We test this directly. \n426\t\n427\t From the permissive corpora the model already trains on, we mine every\n428\tclip whose caption names vocal emotion or paralinguistic vocalization,\n429\tusing a keyword list covering affect terms and vocalizations, and\n430\texcluding obvious non-vocal senses. This yields 29,428 clips\n431\t(Table  3 ) with 25,392 distinct captions, four times\n432\t Crema-D ’s size, drawn from seven sources. Caption diversity is far\n433\thigher than Crema-D ’s ( ∼ \\sim 1.2 clips per caption versus ∼ \\sim 20), so\n434\tin-batch false negatives are less of a concern here, not more. \n435\t\n436\t source \n437\t clips \n438\t\n439\t WavCaps / AudioSet-SL \n440\t 11,040 \n441\t\n442\t AudioCaps \n443\t 5,691 \n444\t\n445\t AudioCaps 2.0 \n446\t 5,040 \n447\t\n448\t FreeSound \n449\t 4,317 \n450\t\n451\t FSD50K \n452\t 1,764 \n453\t\n454\t FreeSound (tail) \n455\t 1,188 \n456\t\n457\t BBC SFX \n458\t 388 \n459\t\n460\t total \n461\t 29,428 \n462\t\n463\t distinct captions \n464\t 25,392 \n465\t\n466\t Table 3: The mined emotion corpus is four times Crema-D ’s size and far\n467\tmore diverse: 29,428 clips from seven sources, 25,392 distinct\n468\tcaptions, every caption naming vocal emotion or a paralinguistic\n469\tvocalization. \n470\t\n471\t fine-tune \n472\t clips \n473\t ep. \n474\t SpeechCommands \n475\t Ravdess \n476\t\n477\t none \n478\t – \n479\t – \n480\t 0.894 \n481\t 0.211 \n482\t\n483\t mined \n484\t 29,428 \n485\t 0.65 \n486\t 0.923 \n487\t 0.216 \n488\t\n489\t mined \n490\t 29,428 \n491\t 2.6 \n492\t 0.929 \n493\t 0.210 \n494\t\n495\t Table 4: Mined emotion-captioned audio leaves emotion unchanged:\n496\t − 0.0007 -0.0007 against the baseline at 2.6 epochs, the exposure at which\n497\t Crema-D produced + 0.297 +0.297 (Table  2 ). \n498\t\n499\t The second mined row of Table  4 is the control that matters. At 2.6 epochs (exactly\n500\tthe exposure at which Crema-D produced + 0.297 +0.297 ) the mined\n501\tcorpus produces − 0.0007 -0.0007 . Four times the data, captions that explicitly\n502\tname the target attribute, an identical recipe, matched optimization, and\n503\tno effect. \n504\t\n505\t Keyword accuracy meanwhile rises to 0.929. The mined set is\n506\tlargely human vocalization, so it functions as further vocal-content\n507\tsupervision. This is consistent with the same competition that produced\n508\tthe original regression in Section  4 : the corpus pushes on the\n509\taxis the model already favors (Figure  1 ). \n510\t\n511\t 7 The deficit is not truncation \n512\t\n513\t Evaluating at full dimensionality recovers only 18% of the emotion gap,\n514\tso the deficit is not information hidden in the truncated rungs. Because\n515\tthe embedding is Matryoshka-nested, a deficit measured at the default\n516\trung could in principle reflect information that is present but\n517\ttruncated. Table  5 evaluates Ravdess at the default and at\n518\tfull dimensionality for both the pre- and post-speech models. \n519\t\n520\t Ravdess \n521\t d = 1024 d{=}1024 \n522\t d = 2048 d{=}2048 \n523\t\n524\t pre-speech \n525\t 0.348 \n526\t 0.337 \n527\t\n528\t post-speech \n529\t 0.211 \n530\t 0.224 \n531\t\n532\t gap \n533\t 0.137 \n534\t 0.113 \n535\t\n536\t Table 5: Full dimensionality recovers about 18% of the gap (0.137 to\n537\t0.113) and slightly lowers the pre-speech model. The prosody\n538\tinformation is not present-but-truncated. \n539\t\n540\t Doubling the read-out closes 18% of the gap and reduces the pre-speech\n541\tmodel’s own score, which is not the signature of information waiting in\n542\tthe upper rungs. \n543\t\n544\t 8 The intervention is otherwise inert \n545\t\n546\t The mined fine-tune leaves a nine-task board essentially unchanged, so\n547\tits null effect on emotion is not masked by broad damage. The remaining\n548\talternative was that the fine-tune degrades the representation\n549\tgenerally; Table  6 rules this out. \n550\t\n551\t task \n552\t base \n553\t mined \n554\t Δ \\Delta \n555\t\n556\t SpeechCommands \n557\t 0.894 \n558\t 0.929 \n559\t + 0.035 +0.035 \n560\t\n561\t MACS T2A \n562\t 0.137 \n563\t 0.150 \n564\t + 0.013 +0.013 \n565\t\n566\t GTZAN reranking \n567\t 0.717 \n568\t 0.721 \n569\t + 0.005 +0.005 \n570\t\n571\t Clotho T2A \n572\t 0.300 \n573\t 0.301 \n574\t + 0.002 +0.002 \n575\t\n576\t VehicleSound clust. \n577\t 0.025 \n578\t 0.025 \n579\t + 0.001 +0.001 \n580\t\n581\t Ravdess \n582\t 0.211 \n583\t 0.210 \n584\t − 0.001 -0.001 \n585\t\n586\t UrbanSound8K T2A \n587\t 0.010 \n588\t 0.009 \n589\t − 0.001 -0.001 \n590\t\n591\t BeijingOpera \n592\t 0.924 \n593\t 0.919 \n594\t − 0.004 -0.004 \n595\t\n596\t GTZAN genre \n597\t 0.644 \n598\t 0.635 \n599\t − 0.009 -0.009 \n600\t\n601\t Table 6: The mined fine-tune is not broadly destructive. Across a\n602\tnine-task board, no cell is degraded by more than 0.009 0.009 ; the only\n603\tgains above that are keyword spotting ( + 0.035 +0.035 ) and MACS text-to-audio\n604\tretrieval ( + 0.013 +0.013 ). Rows are ordered by Δ \\Delta . \n605\t\n606\t The mined corpus is therefore not harming the representation. It simply\n607\tdoes not teach prosody. \n608\t\n609\t Figure 3: An attribute is encoded only when the negatives cannot be\n610\tseparated without it. Left: in the prosody-controlled corpus, lexical\n611\tcontent is held fixed, so prosody is the only remaining signal and the\n612\tobjective cannot be minimized without encoding it. Right: in mined\n613\tnatural captions, scenes differ, so items are separable by scene content\n614\talone; emotion is named in the caption but never required . \n615\t\n616\t 9 Caption separability predicts what is learned \n617\t\n618\t A text-side statistic, computed from the corpus alone with no training,\n619\torders the corpora exactly as their outcomes do. The account above\n620\timplies such a property: if the text side alone resolves an anchor from\n621\tits negatives, the audio side is never forced to carry the attribute. We\n622\ttherefore measure, directly from the cached text embeddings, how\n623\tconfusable an item’s caption is with the captions of its negatives. \n624\t\n625\t For sampled negative sets of 128 items we report two statistics\n626\t(Table  7 ): collision rate , the fraction of anchors\n627\twhose exact caption also appears on another item in the set; and\n628\t mean max similarity , the average cosine similarity between an\n629\tanchor’s caption and its most similar distractor. Both are properties of\n630\tthe corpus, not of any trained model. \n631\t\n632\t corpus \n633\t clips/cap. \n634\t coll. \n635\t max sim \n636\t\n637\t Crema-D \n638\t 19.90 \n639\t 0.556 \n640\t 0.971 \n641\t\n642\t mined emotion \n643\t 1.16 \n644\t 0.038 \n645\t 0.827 \n646\t\n647\t AudioCaps \n648\t 1.09 \n649\t 0.003 \n650\t 0.804 \n651\t\n652\t spoken words \n653\t 5.53 \n654\t 0.006 \n655\t 0.759 \n656\t\n657\t Table 7: Text-side separability singles out the one corpus that taught\n658\tprosody. In Crema-D , 55.6% of anchors have an exact caption duplicate\n659\tamong their negatives and the nearest distractor sits at cosine 0.971:\n660\tthe text side cannot resolve the positive. Every other corpus resolves\n661\teasily. \n662\t\n663\t Crema-D , the only corpus that taught prosody, is an outlier by more\n664\tthan an order of magnitude in collision rate. The mined corpus, whose\n665\tcaptions all name emotion, is 14 × \\times less confusable and did not\n666\tteach it. The spoken-word corpus that produced the original regression is\n667\tthe least confusable of all: single-word captions are semantically\n668\tfar apart, so the text side resolves everything and the audio side need\n669\tonly encode which word was said. \n670\t\n671\t This inverts a standard quality heuristic. Caption diversity is normally\n672\ta virtue, and by that measure Crema-D is the worst corpus in the table.\n673\tIt is the only one that works, because low diversity is what forces the\n674\taudio side to do the discriminating. We suggest reporting these\n675\tstatistics alongside corpus size when a contrastive corpus is introduced,\n676\tsince they are free to compute and predict which attributes the corpus\n677\tcan teach. \n678\t\n679\t 10 Mechanism \n680\t\n681\t We propose that a contrastive objective encodes an attribute only when\n682\tits negative set cannot be separated without it (Figure  3 ). \n683\t\n684\t The negative set is not the batch. \n685\t\n686\t Our objective draws\n687\tnegatives from the batch together with a FIFO memory bank of\n688\tfrozen text embeddings, so the relevant comparison set is far larger than\n689\ta single batch and is drawn from the corpus at large. This matters for\n690\tthe argument, and it sharpens rather than weakens it: the question is not\n691\twhether 128 sampled items happen to be confusable, but whether the\n692\t corpus supplies text-side negatives that make the attribute\n693\tnecessary. A corpus with few distinct captions is confusable everywhere,\n694\tnot just occasionally. \n695\t\n696\t In Crema-D , sentence content is fixed by construction, and the corpus\n697\tcontains only 374 distinct captions across 7,442 clips. Any negative\n698\tdrawn from the bank is therefore likely to carry near-identical text to\n699\tthe anchor. The text side cannot resolve the positive, so the loss is not\n700\tminimizable without representing prosody. The attribute is\n701\t required . \n702\t\n703\t In the mined corpus, an item such as “a man yelling and ducks in the\n704\tbackground” does name the emotional attribute, but its 25,392 distinct\n705\tcaptions mean almost every negative differs in scene content. The item is\n706\tseparable by scene alone (ducks, vehicles, water), so the loss\n707\treaches its minimum without ever encoding how the voice sounds. The\n708\tattribute is mentioned but not required . \n709\t\n710\t Note that this makes caption diversity, ordinarily a marker of corpus\n711\tquality, the very property that lets the model skip the attribute.\n712\tSection  9 makes that quantitative. \n713\t\n714\t This account explains the failure of scale in Section  6 , the\n715\tsuccess of a much smaller corpus in Section  5 , and the\n716\toriginal regression in Section  4 : a corpus of isolated spoken\n717\twords makes lexical identity the discriminative axis, so prosody is not\n718\tmerely unsupervised but selected against. \n719\t\n720\t A testable prediction. \n721\t\n722\t If the mechanism is correct, the mined\n723\tcorpus should encode emotion once emotion is made the residual\n724\tdiscriminative axis, with no new audio. Section  11 tests this\n725\tdirectly, first under the most literal reading (regroup the batches) and\n726\tthen under the sharper one the mechanism actually implies (remove the\n727\ttext-side shortcut so the negatives cannot be separated without emotion). \n728\t\n729\t 11 Testing the mechanism \n730\t\n731\t Restructuring the negatives of the mined corpus recovers emotion with no\n732\tnew audio, exactly as the mechanism predicts: emotion returns when\n733\tprosody becomes the only way to tell training items apart, and not\n734\tbefore. We test this on the\n735\t identical mined clips, holding the fine-tuning recipe of\n736\tSection  3 fixed and changing only the structure of the\n737\tcontrastive negatives. Both interventions initialize from the post-speech\n738\tmodel and run to the same 2.6-epoch exposure as Section  6 .\n739\tFigure  4 summarizes the outcome. \n740\t\n741\t Figure 4: Caption collision, not caption similarity, recovers emotion.\n742\tThe same mined audio is trained under four corpus structures ( Ravdess \n743\tzero-shot emotion): grouping batches by scene similarity (E6a) does not\n744\tmove emotion, while collapsing caption diversity so that emotion is the\n745\trequired discriminative axis (E6b) recovers it most of the way to the\n746\tpre-speech level (dashed), with none of the audio changed. Crema-D , a\n747\tdifferent, prosody-controlled audio corpus, is shown for reference. \n748\t\n749\t 11.1 Similarity is not enough \n750\t\n751\t Scene-grouped batching raises caption similarity and leaves emotion\n752\tunmoved. The most literal reading of the mechanism is to group each batch by scene. We order the\n753\tcorpus so that every batch of 128 is a nearest-neighbor cluster in the\n754\tspace of emotion-stripped caption embeddings, which raises the mean\n755\twithin-batch caption similarity from 0.65 0.65 to 0.71 0.71 (nearest-distractor\n756\tcosine 0.83 → 0.89 0.83\\to 0.89 ) while keeping 6.6 6.6 emotions per batch on average,\n757\tand we draw negatives from the batch alone. Table  8 shows the\n758\tresult: scene-grouped batching does not recover emotion ( Ravdess 0.222 0.222 ,\n759\tagainst a random-batch control of 0.231 0.231 and a pre-intervention baseline\n760\tof 0.216 0.216 ). \n761\t\n762\t in-batch negatives \n763\t Ravdess \n764\t SpeechCommands \n765\t\n766\t post-speech base \n767\t 0.216 \n768\t 0.915 \n769\t\n770\t random control \n771\t 0.231 \n772\t 0.926 \n773\t\n774\t scene-grouped \n775\t 0.222 \n776\t 0.931 \n777\t\n778\t Table 8: Scene-grouped batching (E6a) leaves emotion unmoved despite\n779\traising the nearest-distractor caption cosine to 0.89 0.89 (within-batch\n780\tmean similarity 0.65 → 0.71 0.65\\to 0.71 ). Grouping raised similarity but not\n781\t collision : captions stayed distinct (exact-duplicate rate 0.04 0.04 ,\n782\tversus Crema-D ’s 0.56 0.56 ), so the text side could still resolve items by\n783\tfine caption differences and prosody was never required. \n784\t\n785\t This sharpens the mechanism. The operative property is not proximity but\n786\t collision : Crema-D works because many items carry the same\n787\tcaption, not because their captions are merely similar. Grouping moved the\n788\tsimilarity statistic of Section  9 without moving the collision\n789\tstatistic, and emotion did not move with it. \n790\t\n791\t 11.2 Collision recovers emotion \n792\t\n793\t Collapsing caption diversity so that emotion is the only separating axis\n794\trecovers it on the same audio (Table  9 ). To remove the\n795\ttext-side shortcut directly, we relabel every mined clip with a Crema-D -style emotion-tone caption (for example\n796\t“Speech with an angry tone, spoken by a person”), assigning the label\n797\tfrom the clip’s dominant emotion keyword, and cache these as the text\n798\ttargets; the audio is untouched. This gives the corpus Crema-D ’s\n799\tstructure: caption diversity collapses from 25 , 392 25{,}392 distinct captions\n800\tto 16 16 , the within-batch collision rate rises from 0.04 0.04 to 0.98 0.98 , and\n801\tthe full-corpus frozen-text bank now makes emotion the only axis that\n802\tseparates an anchor from its many same-caption negatives. The control is\n803\tthe identical run on the original scene-descriptive captions. \n804\t\n805\t mined captions \n806\t Ravdess \n807\t SpeechCommands \n808\t\n809\t scene-descriptive \n810\t 0.221 \n811\t 0.930 \n812\t\n813\t emotion-tone \n814\t 0.310 \n815\t 0.903 \n816\t\n817\t Crema-D audio (ref) \n818\t 0.508 \n819\t 0.845 \n820\t\n821\t Table 9: Caption collision (E6b): coarsening the mined captions from\n822\t 25 , 392 25{,}392 distinct to 16 16 Crema-D -style emotion-tone labels makes\n823\temotion the required axis on the same mined audio, recovering\n824\t Ravdess by + 0.089 +0.089 , most of the way back to the pre-speech level\n825\t( 0.348 0.348 ), while keyword accuracy trades back. Three seeds each, random\n826\tbatching with the full-corpus text bank: control 0.221 0.221 \n827\t( 0.217 / 0.224 / 0.222 0.217/0.224/0.222 ), collision 0.310 0.310 ( 0.308 / 0.308 / 0.315 0.308/0.308/0.315 ), positive\n828\tat every seed with non-overlapping ranges. Crema-D audio ( 374 374 \n829\tcaptions) is shown for reference; only the caption structure differs\n830\tbetween the two mined rows. \n831\t\n832\t The same clips that produced no effect as scene descriptions\n833\t(Section  6 ) recover emotion once relabeled so that emotion is\n834\trequired (Table  9 ). The effect is large ( + 0.089 +0.089 Ravdess ) and holds at every seed\n835\twith non-overlapping ranges, and it is not free: keyword accuracy falls\n836\tfrom 0.930 0.930 to 0.903 0.903 , the same competition that produced the original\n837\tregression in Section  4 , now running in reverse. The recovery\n838\tis partial ( 0.310 0.310 against Crema-D ’s 0.508 0.508 on the same eval),\n839\tconsistent with the mined vocalization clips carrying a noisier prosodic\n840\tsignal than Crema-D ’s controlled recordings. \n841\t\n842\t A second, non-acted benchmark. \n843\t\n844\t On Iemocap (Busso et al., 2008 ) emotion\n845\tclassification (a different corpus, improvised rather than lexically\n846\tmatched), the same recovery appears in sign but attenuated: the collision\n847\tmodel scores 0.223 0.223 against the scene-caption control’s 0.204 0.204 and the\n848\tbaseline’s 0.201 0.201 (three-seed means; the gap is positive at every seed\n849\twith non-overlapping ranges, but roughly a quarter of the Ravdess effect).\n850\tThe main effect is therefore strong within the acted-emotion paradigm and\n851\tdirectionally, seed-robustly confirmed outside it, not uniform across\n852\temotion corpora. \n853\t\n854\t 12 Discussion \n855\t\n856\t The practical reading is that “add data containing the attribute” is\n857\tnot a well-formed instruction for contrastive training. The mined corpus\n858\tsatisfies that instruction completely (every clip’s caption names\n859\tvocal emotion), and it does nothing. The controlled corpus violates the\n860\tusual desiderata for corpus quality, in that it has only 374 distinct\n861\tcaptions across 7,442 clips and therefore a high false-negative rate,\n862\tand it works. \n863\t\n864\t This inverts the usual quality heuristics. Caption diversity, corpus\n865\tsize, and label coverage are all proxies for the thing that matters, and\n866\tin this case they are anti-correlated with it. What matters is whether\n867\tthe corpus makes the target attribute necessary for discrimination. \n868\t\n869\t A corollary worth stating: this predicts that mixing a controlled corpus\n870\tinto a large natural one may dilute exactly the property that makes it\n871\twork, since the controlled items would be batched against natural\n872\tnegatives and become separable without prosody. If so, the intervention\n873\tbelongs at the batch level rather than the corpus level. We have not\n874\ttested this. \n875\t\n876\t 13 Limitations \n877\t\n878\t The fine-tuning arms of Sections  4 through  8 are\n879\tsingle runs; only the interventions of Section  11 are\n880\treplicated across three seeds. The single-run effect sizes are large\n881\trelative to typical seed variance in this setup, but we do not report\n882\tseed replicates for them, and the quantitative exchange rates in\n883\tSection  5 should be read as approximate rather than measured. \n884\t\n885\t The emotion axis rests principally on a single benchmark, and the two\n886\tcorpora share\n887\tmore structure than is ideal for a clean test. Crema-D and Ravdess are\n888\tboth acted corpora with categorical labels, and both hold lexical\n889\tcontent approximately fixed across emotions: Livingstone and Russo ( 2018 ) \n890\tuse lexically-matched statements by design, exactly the property we argue\n891\tmakes Crema-D effective. Section  5 is therefore partly\n892\tdomain transfer, and the recovery may be assisted by the evaluation\n893\tsharing the training corpus’s structural bias. We therefore report a\n894\tsecond, non-acted emotion benchmark, Iemocap , on which the\n895\tstructural recovery of Section  11 replicates in sign but at a\n896\tmuch smaller magnitude.\n897\tThe main effect should be read as strong within the acted-emotion\n898\tparadigm and directionally confirmed outside it, not as uniform across\n899\temotion corpora; the mined vocalization corpus transfers imperfectly to\n900\tconversational emotion. \n901\t\n902\t The keyword gains in Section  4 follow from training on\n903\tspoken-word data. This is a genuine capability gain but it is not\n904\tzero-shot generalization in the strict sense for this model, and we do\n905\tnot claim it as such. \n906\t\n907\t The mechanism is no longer only an inference from the contrast between\n908\ttwo corpora. Section  11 tests it directly on the same audio:\n909\traising in-batch caption similarity leaves emotion unmoved, while\n910\tcollapsing caption diversity so that emotion is the required\n911\tdiscriminative axis recovers it. We are deliberate about what this does\n912\tand does not show. The emotion-tone relabeling is itself a supervised\n913\tsignal, and a reader could call it a relabeling into a classification\n914\ttask. The point is that the same signal, present as free text in\n915\tthe mined captions of Section  6 , produced no effect, and\n916\tbecame usable only once the corpus structure made it necessary. What the\n917\tintervention isolates is that structure, not the mere presence of the\n918\tlabel, is the operative variable. It remains a single base model and a\n919\tsingle audio pipeline; whether the effect is objective-level rather than\n920\tarchitecture-specific rests on the model-agnostic prior results we cite\n921\tin Section  2 . \n922\t\n923\t Data contamination. \n924\t\n925\t Our pretraining corpus includes 70K\n926\tCommon Voice clips sampled from the validated pool. In Common\n927\tVoice, validated is the superset from which train, dev, and test\n928\tsplits are drawn, and the corpus is cumulative across releases. Replaying\n929\tour (deterministic) ingest to recover the exact sampled clip identifiers,\n930\twe measure 44 of 200 (22%) of the CommonVoice v21 mini test\n931\tclips present in our training set , alongside 47% verbatim sentence\n932\toverlap. The cause is structural rather than accidental: the ingest\n933\tstreams the archive in order and stops at a target count, archive order\n934\ttracks clip identifier, and low identifiers are the older recordings that\n935\tthe v21 test split draws from, so “the first N N that pass the filters”\n936\tsystematically over-samples the evaluation era. The effect is compounded\n937\tbecause our spoken-word corpus is itself derived from Common Voice. We\n938\ttherefore do not report any Common Voice retrieval result for these\n939\tmodels, and the evaluations in this paper are drawn from tasks whose\n940\tsource corpora we do not train on. \n941\t\n942\t We flag the general hazard, since it is not specific to us: several\n943\tcorpora in wide use as training data (Common Voice, Crema-D ,\n944\tIEMOCAP, VoxPopuli, FLEURS, GigaSpeech) are simultaneously tasks \n945\tin audio embedding benchmarks. A model trained on the obvious speech data\n946\tis contaminated on several cells by default, and sampling from a pooled\n947\t“validated” split rather than a designated train split makes this\n948\tlikely rather than possible. Corpora intended for training should record\n949\tsource clip identifiers so that overlap can be audited after the fact;\n950\tours did not, and recovering them required replaying the ingest. \n951\t\n952\t Finally, Crema-D is distributed under a share-alike database license\n953\tand is used here for controlled experimentation only. No model weights\n954\ttrained on it are released, and no model trained on it is submitted to a\n955\tbenchmark whose tasks derive from it. \n956\t\n957\t 14 Conclusion \n958\t\n959\t Adding a large lexical-speech round to a contrastive audio embedding\n960\timproved keyword spotting by 76 points and cost 14 points of emotion\n961\trecognition. The lost attribute was recovered by a corpus 120 × 120\\times \n962\tsmaller than the training corpus, whose construction made prosody the\n963\tonly way to tell training items apart, and was not recovered at all by a\n964\tcorpus four times larger\n965\twhose captions named emotion explicitly but whose items remained\n966\tseparable without it. A direct intervention closes the loop: restructuring\n967\tthat same mined corpus so that emotion is the only axis separating\n968\ttraining items recovers it, while raising in-batch similarity alone does\n969\tnot, so the structural property is a cause and not a correlate. What a\n970\tcontrastive corpus teaches is decided by what its negatives make\n971\tnecessary. Data volume and caption vocabulary are poor proxies for that,\n972\tand optimizing them is not a substitute for designing it. To give a\n973\tcontrastive embedding an attribute, construct the corpus so that its\n974\tnegatives cannot be separated without that attribute. \n975\t\n976\t References \n977\t\n978\t Bleeker et al. (2024) \n979\t\n980\tMaurits Bleeker, Mariya Hendriksen, Andrew Yates, and Maarten de Rijke. 2024.\n981\t\n982\t Demonstrating and reducing shortcuts in vision-language representation learning.\n983\t\n984\t In Transactions on Machine Learning Research (TMLR) .\n985\t\n986\t ArXiv:2402.17510.\n987\t\n988\t Busso et al. (2008) \n989\t\n990\tCarlos Busso, Murtaza Bulut, Chi-Chun Lee, Abe Kazemzadeh, Emily Mower, Samuel Kim, Jeannette N. Chang, Sungbok Lee, and Shrikanth S. Narayanan. 2008.\n991\t\n992\t IEMOCAP: Interactive emotional dyadic motion capture database.\n993\t\n994\t Language Resources and Evaluation , 42(4):335–359.\n995\t\n996\t Cao et al. (2014) \n997\t\n998\tHouwei Cao, David G. Cooper, Michael K. Keutmann, Ruben C. Gur, Ani Nenkova, and Ragini Verma. 2014.\n999\t\n1000\t CREMA-D: Crowd-sourced emotional multimodal actors dataset .\n1001\t\n1002\t IEEE Transactions on Affective Computing , 5(4):377–390.\n1003\t\n1004\t Chen et al. (2021) \n1005\t\n1006\tTing Chen, Calvin Luo, and Lala Li. 2021.\n1007\t\n1008\t Intriguing properties of contrastive losses.\n1009\t\n1010\t In Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS) .\n1011\t\n1012\t ArXiv:2011.02803.\n1013\t\n1014\t Elizalde et al. (2023) \n1015\t\n1016\tBenjamin Elizalde, Soham Deshmukh, Mahmoud Al Ismail, and Huaming Wang. 2023.\n1017\t\n1018\t CLAP: Learning audio concepts from natural language supervision.\n1019\t\n1020\t In IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP) .\n1021\t\n1022\t ArXiv:2206.04769.\n1023\t\n1024\t Geirhos et al. (2020) \n1025\t\n1026\tRobert Geirhos, Jörn-Henrik Jacobsen, Claudio Michaelis, Richard Zemel, Wieland Brendel, Matthias Bethge, and Felix A. Wichmann. 2020.\n1027\t\n1028\t Shortcut learning in deep neural networks.\n1029\t\n1030\t arXiv preprint arXiv:2004.07780 .\n1031\t\n1032\t Kusupati et al. (2022) \n1033\t\n1034\tAditya Kusupati, Gantavya Bhatt, Aniket Rege, Matthew Wallingford, Aditya Sinha, Vivek Ramanujan, William Howard-Snyder, Kaifeng Chen, Sham Kakade, Prateek Jain, and Ali Farhadi. 2022.\n1035\t\n1036\t Matryoshka representation learning.\n1037\t\n1038\t arXiv preprint arXiv:2205.13147 .\n1039\t\n1040\t Livingstone and Russo (2018) \n1041\t\n1042\tSteven R. Livingstone and Frank A. Russo. 2018.\n1043\t\n1044\t The Ryerson audio-visual database of emotional speech and song (RAVDESS): A dynamic, multimodal set of facial and vocal expressions in North American English .\n1045\t\n1046\t PLoS ONE , 13(5):e0196391.\n1047\t\n1048\t Martel et al. (2026) \n1049\t\n1050\tHéctor Martel, Joe Hennessy-Priest, and Taemin Cho. 2026.\n1051\t\n1052\t Probing low-level acoustic attribute encoding in CLAP audio embeddings.\n1053\t\n1054\t Proc. International Conference on Digital Audio Effects (DAFx) .\n1055\t\n1056\t ArXiv:2607.03806.\n1057\t\n1058\t Mei et al. (2023) \n1059\t\n1060\tXinhao Mei, Chutong Meng, Haohe Liu, Qiuqiang Kong, Tom Ko, Chengqi Zhao, Mark D. Plumbley, and Yuexian Zou. 2023.\n1061\t\n1062\t WavCaps: A ChatGPT-assisted weakly-labelled audio captioning dataset for audio-language multimodal research.\n1063\t\n1064\t arXiv preprint arXiv:2303.17395 .\n1065\t\n1066\t Muennighoff et al. (2022) \n1067\t\n1068\tNiklas Muennighoff, Nouamane Tazi, Loïc Magne, and Nils Reimers. 2022.\n1069\t\n1070\t MTEB: Massive text embedding benchmark.\n1071\t\n1072\t arXiv preprint arXiv:2210.07316 .\n1073\t\n1074\t Pan et al. (2023) \n1075\t\n1076\tYu Pan, Yanni Hu, Yuguang Yang, Wen Fei, Jixun Yao, Heng Lu, Lei Ma, and Jianjun Zhao. 2023.\n1077\t\n1078\t GEmo-CLAP: Gender-attribute-enhanced contrastive language-audio pretraining for accurate speech emotion recognition.\n1079\t\n1080\t arXiv preprint arXiv:2306.07848 .\n1081\t\n1082\t Robinson et al. (2020) \n1083\t\n1084\tJoshua Robinson, Ching-Yao Chuang, Suvrit Sra, and Stefanie Jegelka. 2020.\n1085\t\n1086\t Contrastive learning with hard negative samples.\n1087\t\n1088\t arXiv preprint arXiv:2010.04592 .\n1089\t\n1090\t Robinson et al. (2021) \n1091\t\n1092\tJoshua Robinson, Li Sun, Ke Yu, Kayhan Batmanghelich, Stefanie Jegelka, and Suvrit Sra. 2021.\n1093\t\n1094\t Can contrastive learning avoid shortcut solutions?\n1095\t\n1096\t arXiv preprint arXiv:2106.11230 .\n1097\t\n1098\t Tonmoy et al. (2026) \n1099\t\n1100\tAbdul Basit Tonmoy, Kazi Fardinul Hoque, Md. Shahrier Islam Arham, and Arman Luthra. 2026.\n1101\t\n1102\t Fusion embedding: A unified embedding space for text, image, video, and audio.\n1103\t\n1104\t arXiv preprint arXiv:2607.18666 .\n1105\t\n1106\t Warden (2018) \n1107\t\n1108\tPete Warden. 2018.\n1109\t\n1110\t Speech commands: A dataset for limited-vocabulary speech recognition.\n1111\t\n1112\t arXiv preprint arXiv:1804.03209 .\n1113\t\n1114\t Wu et al. (2022) \n1115\t\n1116\tYusong Wu, Ke Chen, Tianyu Zhang, Yuchen Hui, Marianna Nezhurina, Taylor Berg-Kirkpatrick, and Shlomo Dubnov. 2022.\n1117\t\n1118\t Large-scale contrastive language-audio pretraining with feature fusion and keyword-to-caption augmentation.\n1119\t\n1120\t arXiv preprint arXiv:2211.06687 .\n1121\t\n1122\t Zhang et al. (2024) \n1123\t\n1124\tJihai Zhang, Xiang Lan, Xiaoye Qu, Yu Cheng, Mengling Feng, and Bryan Hooi. 2024.\n1125\t\n1126\t Learning the unlearned: Mitigating feature suppression in contrastive learning.\n1127\t\n1128\t In European Conference on Computer Vision (ECCV) .\n1129\t\n1130\t ArXiv:2402.11816.\n1131\t\n1132\t Experimental support, please\n1133\t view the build logs \n1134\t for errors. Generated by\n1135\t\n1136\t L\n1137\t A \n1138\t T\n1139\t E \n1140\t\n1141\t xml \n1142\t\n1143\t .\n1144\t\n1145\t Instructions for reporting errors \n1146\t We are continuing to improve HTML versions of papers, and your feedback helps enhance accessibility and mobile\n1147\t support. To report errors in the HTML that will help us improve conversion and rendering, choose any of the\n1148\t methods listed below: \n1149\t\n1150\t Click the \"Report Issue\" ( \n1151\t\n1152\t ) button, located in the page header. \n1153\t\n1154\t Tip: You can select the relevant text first, to include it in your report. \n1155\t Our team has already identified the following issues . We appreciate your time reviewing and reporting rendering errors we\n1156\t may not have found yet. Your efforts will help us improve the HTML versions for all readers, because disability\n1157\t should not be a barrier to accessing research. Thank you for your continued support in championing open access for\n1158\t all. \n1159\t Have a free development cycle? Help support accessibility at arXiv! Our collaborators at LaTeXML maintain a list of packages that need conversion , and welcome developer contributions . \n1160\t\n1161\t We gratefully acknowledge support from\n1162\t our major funders ,\n1163\t member institutions , ,\n1164\t and all contributors.\n1165\t\n1166\t About \n1167\t &middot; \n1168\t Help \n1169\t &middot; \n1170\t Contact \n1171\t &middot; \n1172\t Subscribe \n1173\t &middot; \n1174\t Copyright \n1175\t &middot; \n1176\t Privacy \n1177\t &middot; \n1178\t Accessibility \n1179\t &middot; \n1180\t Operational Status (opens in new tab) \n1181\t\n1182\t Major funding support from \n1183\t\n1184\t\n1185\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "7959668d-df83-4761-87bb-619bb93714f6", "uuid": "a5e096a4-84e7-4788-b4db-741fd45bfcb2", "timestamp": "2026-08-04T05:54:49.822Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io/.claude-review/projects/-inspire-qb-ilm-project-video-generation-chenxie-25019-hyr-iHateTheWorld555-github-io/7959668d-df83-4761-87bb-619bb93714f6/tool-results/bxdnlez22.txt", "content": " \n\n Discriminative Axis, Not Data Volume: What a Contrastive Corpus Teaches an Audio Embedding \n\n Report GitHub Issue \n × \n\n Title: \n\n Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: \n\n Description: \n\n Submit without GitHub \n Submit in GitHub \n\n arXiv is now an independent nonprofit! \n Learn more \n &times; \n\n Back to arXiv \n\n Why HTML? \n\n Report Issue \n\n Back to Abstract \n\n Download PDF \n\n Abstract \n 1 Introduction \n\n 2 Related work \n\n Shortcut learning and feature suppression. \n Hard negatives and batch composition. \n Audio–text contrastive embeddings. \n\n 3 Setup \n\n Model. \n Pretraining corpus. \n Fine-tuning. \n Evaluation. \n\n 4 Lexical speech trades emotion for keywords \n 5 The deficit is not capacity \n 6 The deficit is not data volume \n 7 The deficit is not truncation \n 8 The intervention is otherwise inert \n 9 Caption separability predicts what is learned \n\n 10 Mechanism \n\n The negative set is not the batch. \n A testable prediction. \n\n 11 Testing the mechanism \n\n 11.1 Similarity is not enough \n\n 11.2 Collision recovers emotion \n\n A second, non-acted benchmark. \n\n 12 Discussion \n\n 13 Limitations \n\n Data contamination. \n\n 14 Conclusion \n References \n\n License: CC BY 4.0\n\narXiv:2608.01560v1 [cs.CL] 03 Aug 2026 \n\n Discriminative Axis, Not Data Volume: What a Contrastive\n Corpus Teaches an Audio Embedding \n\n Abdul Basit Tonmoy \n Eximius Labs  Wabash College \n atonmoy27@wabash.edu \n\n Abstract \n Scaling the corpus is the default remedy when a contrastive\nrepresentation lacks an attribute. We report a case where it does\nnothing, and identify what does: adding a lexical-speech round to a\nfrozen-base multimodal embedding model raises zero-shot keyword spotting\nby 76 points while reducing speech-emotion recognition by 14.\nThe loss is not a capacity limit: fine-tuning on 7,442 clips from a\nprosody-controlled corpus recovers emotion past its pre-speech level at\na five-point keyword cost. Nor is it data volume: 29,428 mined clips\nwhose captions explicitly name emotions, at matched exposure, move\nemotion by − 0.0007 -0.0007 . The difference is structural: a contrastive\nobjective encodes an attribute only when the in-batch negatives cannot\nbe separated without it; the controlled corpus holds sentence content\nfixed, so prosody is the only separating signal, whereas mined captions\nname emotion yet remain separable by scene content. Intervention on the\nsame audio confirms causality: raising caption similarity does not\nrecover emotion, but collapsing caption diversity so that emotion\nbecomes the only separating axis recovers it by 8.9 points across three\nseeds, with a smaller, same-signed gain on a non-acted corpus, while\nkeyword accuracy trades back. Corpus structure, not size or caption\nvocabulary, controls what a contrastive audio embedding encodes. \n\n Discriminative Axis, Not Data Volume: What a Contrastive\n Corpus Teaches an Audio Embedding \n\n Abdul Basit Tonmoy \n\n Eximius Labs  Wabash College \n\n atonmoy27@wabash.edu \n\n Figure 1: Corpus structure, not data volume, decides what a contrastive\naudio embedding encodes. Adding a lexical-speech round to a frozen-base\nmultimodal embedding model raises zero-shot keyword spotting by 76\npoints ( 0.133 → 0.894 0.133\\to 0.894 ) while reducing emotion recognition by 14\n( 0.348 → 0.211 0.348\\to 0.211 , the grey baseline shown here). Arrows run from that\nbaseline to each fine-tuned arm: 7,442 prosody-controlled clips\n( Crema-D ) recover emotion past its pre-speech level (dashed) at a cost\nof five keyword points, while a mined corpus four times larger, whose\ncaptions all name emotion explicitly, moves emotion by − 0.0007 -0.0007 at\nmatched exposure. Both axes are zero-shot accuracy. \n\n 1 Introduction \n\n When a learned representation underperforms on some attribute, the\nstandard response is to add data containing that attribute. We report a\nsetting where that response fails cleanly: extending the training corpus\nof an audio embedding model with large quantities of lexical speech\nraised zero-shot keyword spotting by 76 points and reduced \nspeech-emotion recognition by 14 (Figure  1 ). Both are\nspeech tasks, evaluated in the same shared space, by the same protocol;\nmore speech data made the model worse at one of them. \n\n Our setting is an audio embedding built by attaching a trained connector\nand modality-gated adapters to a frozen vision-language embedding model  (Tonmoy et al., 2026 ) .\nWe treat the regression as an opportunity rather than a defect, because\nit permits a sharper question than “does more data help”: which \nproperty of a contrastive corpus causes an attribute to be encoded? We\neliminate three explanations in turn. The deficit is not capacity:\nfine-tuning on 7,442 clips from a prosody-controlled corpus restores\nemotion past its pre-speech level (Section  5 ). It is not data\nvolume: 29,428 mined clips whose captions explicitly name emotion, at\nmatched exposure, move emotion by − 0.0007 -0.0007 (Section  6 ). It\nis not representational truncation, and the mined fine-tune causes no\ncollateral damage that could mask an effect (Sections  7 \nand  8 ). \n\n What remains is that a contrastive objective is indifferent to\nattributes it does not need. An attribute is learned when the in-batch\nnegatives cannot be separated without it, and ignored when they can,\neven if every caption in the corpus names it explicitly. Corpus\nconstruction, not corpus size, decides this; a separability statistic\ncomputable from the corpus alone predicts the outcome\n(Section  9 ), and an intervention on the same audio confirms\nthe account causally (Section  11 ). \n\n Our contributions are: \n\n • \n\n Large-scale lexical-speech supervision trades emotion\nrecognition for keyword recognition in a shared audio embedding,\nrather than improving speech understanding uniformly: + 76 +76 keyword\npoints, − 14 -14 emotion points (Table  1 ,\nSection  4 ). \n\n • \n\n The loss is not capacity-bound: a corpus 120 × 120\\times smaller than\nthe post-speech training corpus restores the lost attribute past its\noriginal level, 0.211 → 0.508 0.211\\to 0.508 against a pre-speech 0.348 0.348 \n(Table  2 , Section  5 ). \n\n • \n\n A matched-exposure negative control shows that four times the\ndata, with captions that explicitly name the target attribute, moves\nemotion by − 0.0007 -0.0007 (Table  4 , Section  6 ). \n\n • \n\n Two further explanations fail: representational truncation\nrecovers only 18% of the gap (Table  5 ,\nSection  7 ), and a nine-task board shows no general\ndegradation that could mask an effect (Table  6 ,\nSection  8 ). \n\n • \n\n A controlled test of the mechanism: restructuring the same corpus\nso that emotion is the required discriminative axis recovers the\nattribute by + 0.089 +0.089 across three seeds, whereas raising in-batch\ncaption similarity alone does not (Tables  8 \nand  9 , Figure  4 , Section  11 ). This\nconverts the account from an observation across corpora into a\ndemonstrated cause. \n\n 2 Related work \n\n Our finding sits at the intersection of three lines of work. \n\n Shortcut learning and feature suppression. \n\n A model trained\nwith a discriminative objective uses whichever feature minimizes the loss\nmost cheaply, and need not represent alternatives\n (Geirhos et al., 2020 ) . In contrastive learning this appears as\n feature suppression : Chen et al. ( 2021 ) construct datasets\nwith explicitly competing features and show that a few bits of\neasy-to-learn shared feature can suppress, and even fully prevent, the\nlearning of competing ones. Robinson et al. ( 2021 ) show the same\ntendency follows from optimizing the InfoNCE objective itself, and\npropose implicit feature modification to counter it. \n\n Both establish the phenomenon by manipulating the inputs \n(synthetic competing features, or altered positives and negatives), and\nrecent work removes it algorithmically: Zhang et al. ( 2024 ) mitigate\nsuppression with feature-aware negative sampling that draws an anchor’s\nnegatives from its own cluster, and Bleeker et al. ( 2024 ) reduce\nvision-language shortcuts with auxiliary reconstruction objectives. These\ntreat suppression as a property of the sampler or loss to engineer away on\na fixed dataset. Our contribution is complementary and, to our knowledge,\nnot made elsewhere: the same suppression is governed by the ordinary\nconstruction of a real training corpus , at scale; it is not\nremedied by adding data in which the suppressed attribute is explicitly\ndescribed; and it is reversed by a structural change to the corpus rather\nthan to the algorithm. We do not claim the phenomenon of suppression is\nnew. We isolate the corpus as its operative variable with a\nmatched-exposure control, and we do so in audio, where CLAP-family corpora\nare usually discussed only in terms of scale and caption quality. \n\n Hard negatives and batch composition. \n\n A related line\nimproves contrastive representations by changing which negatives appear\nin a batch, on the argument that easy negatives supply little gradient\nsignal (Robinson et al., 2020 ) . That work treats batch composition as an\noptimization device for a fixed target. We make a different claim: batch\ncomposition determines which attribute is learned at all , not\nmerely how efficiently a fixed objective converges. The prediction in\nSection  10 connects the two, since it proposes recovering an\nattribute purely by regrouping items already present in the corpus. \n\n Audio–text contrastive embeddings. \n\n Models in the CLAP family\nalign audio with free-form text and are evaluated zero-shot by embedding\ncandidate class names (Elizalde et al., 2023 ; Wu et al., 2022 ) . Corpus\nconstruction in this literature is discussed largely in terms of scale,\ncaption quality, and noise filtering; Mei et al. ( 2023 ) , for\ninstance, addresses caption quality at scale. Emotion in particular has\nbeen built into CLAP-style models with explicit attribute supervision\n (Pan et al., 2023 ) ; our question is orthogonal, namely when generic\nemotion-captioned data does and does not cause emotion to be encoded at\nall. Probing studies of what a fixed CLAP embedding already contains\nrecover low-level acoustic attributes such as loudness and pitch\n (Martel et al., 2026 ) ; we instead intervene on the corpus and study a\nhigher-level attribute whose encoding we show to be corpus-structure\ndependent. To our knowledge the structural property we isolate, whether\na corpus makes an attribute necessary for discrimination, has\nnot been separated from scale and caption content with a matched-exposure\ncontrol. \n\n We do not claim that attribute competition is novel. The contribution is\nthe controlled comparison that isolates corpus structure as the operative\nvariable, in a setting where the intuitive remedy (more data, explicitly\ncaptioned) demonstrably fails. \n\n 3 Setup \n\n Model. \n\n A 2B-parameter frozen vision–language embedding\nmodel with a trained audio connector (a query-based resampler) and\nmodality-gated deep adapters attached to each decoder layer. The gate is\nclosed on non-audio inputs, so text, image, and video paths execute the\nbase computation graph unchanged. All training in this paper updates only\nthe connector and adapters; the base weights are never modified. The\nembedding is Matryoshka-nested (Kusupati et al., 2022 ) with a\ndefault read-out of 1024\ndimensions. \n\n Pretraining corpus. \n\n 888,800 clips across eleven sources.\nThe lexical-speech round introduced for this study contributes\n ∼ \\sim 150K spoken-word clips, 70K read-speech clips, and a read-audiobook\nset; the remaining sources are environmental audio and audio–caption\npairs. Pretraining uses a frozen-text memory bank, false-negative\nmasking, and soft labels. \n\n Fine-tuning. \n\n All fine-tunes in Sections  5 \nand  6 use an identical recipe, differing only in the corpus\nand the step count: batch 128, no gradient accumulation, peak learning\nrate 3 × 10 − 5 3{\\times}10^{-5} , false-negative masking and soft labels disabled,\ninitialized from the same post-speech checkpoint. Holding the recipe\nfixed is what makes the two corpora comparable. \n\n Evaluation. \n\n We report two zero-shot classification tasks from\nthe MAEB audio embedding benchmark in the mteb framework\n (Muennighoff et al., 2022 ) : SpeechCommands \n (Warden, 2018 ) (35-way keyword spotting)\nand Ravdess (speech emotion). Both are scored by embedding the audio and\nthe candidate class texts into the shared space and matching; neither is\nfine-tuned on. Section  8 additionally reports a nine-task\nboard spanning retrieval, classification, clustering, and reranking, to\ntest for collateral effects. \n\n 4 Lexical speech trades emotion for keywords \n\n Adding the lexical-speech round raises keyword spotting by 76 points and\nlowers emotion recognition by 14. Table  1 reports the two\ntasks before and after the round is added to the pretraining corpus. \n\n SpeechCommands \n Ravdess \n\n pre-speech \n 0.133 \n 0.348 \n\n post-speech \n 0.894 \n 0.211 \n\n Δ \\Delta \n + 0.761 +0.761 \n − 0.137 -0.137 \n\n Table 1: The lexical-speech round trades one speech attribute for\nanother: keyword spotting improves by 76 points while emotion\nrecognition regresses by 14. Both tasks are zero-shot; bold marks the\nbetter value in each column. \n\n The keyword gain is unsurprising: the spoken-word corpus pairs isolated\nwords with their transcriptions, which closely matches the form of the\nevaluation. The emotion regression is the finding. Both tasks are speech;\nan account in which “speech data improves speech understanding”\npredicts the wrong sign for one of them. \n\n Two explanations are available. Either the model lacks the capacity to\nrepresent both lexical content and prosody and the new supervision\nconsumed it, or prosody was never required by the objective and the new\nsupervision actively selected against it. The next two sections separate\nthese. \n\n 5 The deficit is not capacity \n\n A corpus 120 × 120\\times smaller than the post-speech training corpus\nrestores the lost attribute past its pre-speech level, so the deficit is\nnot a capacity limit. Crema-D (Cao et al., 2014 ) contains 7,442\nclips in which 91 actors speak twelve fixed sentences across six\nemotions. Because lexical content is held approximately constant by\nconstruction, two clips drawn into the same batch typically differ in\nspeaker and emotion but not in what is said. \n\n We fine-tune the post-speech model on Crema-D for 150 and 400 steps\n(Table  2 , Figure  2 ). \n\n Figure 2: Only the prosody-controlled corpus recovers emotion. Both\nfine-tuning corpora are shown at two exposures each, against the\npost-speech baseline: Crema-D (7,442 clips) lifts emotion past its\npre-speech level of 0.348 (dashed) at a modest keyword cost, while the\nmined corpus (29,428 clips, every caption naming emotion explicitly)\nleaves emotion unchanged and raises keyword accuracy. Both metrics are\nzero-shot accuracy and share one axis. \n\n fine-tune \n clips \n ep. \n SpeechCommands \n Ravdess \n\n none \n – \n – \n 0.894 \n 0.211 \n\n Crema-D \n 7,442 \n 2.6 \n 0.845 \n 0.508 \n\n Crema-D \n 7,442 \n 6.9 \n 0.832 \n 0.537 \n\n Table 2: A prosody-controlled corpus 120 × 120\\times smaller than the\npost-speech training corpus restores emotion past its pre-speech level\nof 0.348, at a cost of five points of keyword accuracy. The Crema-D \nrows are 150- and 400-step fine-tunes of the post-speech model. \n\n Emotion recovers to 0.508 after 150 steps, exceeding the pre-speech value\nof 0.348, while keyword accuracy falls only to 0.845, still more than\nsix times the pre-speech keyword score. The exchange rate is roughly six\nemotion points per keyword point at 150 steps, degrading to about two\nbetween 150 and 400, so the shorter schedule is the better operating\npoint. \n\n This rules out the capacity account. The model can represent prosody; a\ncorpus two orders of magnitude smaller than the post-speech training\ncorpus is sufficient to reinstate it. What the lexical-speech round did\nwas not exhaust capacity but fail to require prosody. \n\n 6 The deficit is not data volume \n\n Four times the data, with captions that explicitly name the target\nattribute, moves emotion by − 0.0007 -0.0007 (Table  4 ); the deficit\nis not data volume either. If prosody merely needed to be present \nin supervision, then any corpus whose captions describe vocal emotion\nshould serve, and more of it should serve better. We test this directly. \n\n From the permissive corpora the model already trains on, we mine every\nclip whose caption names vocal emotion or paralinguistic vocalization,\nusing a keyword list covering affect terms and vocalizations, and\nexcluding obvious non-vocal senses. This yields 29,428 clips\n(Table  3 ) with 25,392 distinct captions, four times\n Crema-D ’s size, drawn from seven sources. Caption diversity is far\nhigher than Crema-D ’s ( ∼ \\sim 1.2 clips per caption versus ∼ \\sim 20), so\nin-batch false negatives are less of a concern here, not more. \n\n source \n clips \n\n WavCaps / AudioSet-SL \n 11,040 \n\n AudioCaps \n 5,691 \n\n AudioCaps 2.0 \n 5,040 \n\n FreeSound \n 4,317 \n\n FSD50K \n 1,764 \n\n FreeSound (tail) \n 1,188 \n\n BBC SFX \n 388 \n\n total \n 29,428 \n\n distinct captions \n 25,392 \n\n Table 3: The mined emotion corpus is four times Crema-D ’s size and far\nmore diverse: 29,428 clips from seven sources, 25,392 distinct\ncaptions, every caption naming vocal emotion or a paralinguistic\nvocalization. \n\n fine-tune \n clips \n ep. \n SpeechCommands \n Ravdess \n\n none \n – \n – \n 0.894 \n 0.211 \n\n mined \n 29,428 \n 0.65 \n 0.923 \n 0.216 \n\n mined \n 29,428 \n 2.6 \n 0.929 \n 0.210 \n\n Table 4: Mined emotion-captioned audio leaves emotion unchanged:\n − 0.0007 -0.0007 against the baseline at 2.6 epochs, the exposure at which\n Crema-D produced + 0.297 +0.297 (Table  2 ). \n\n The second mined row of Table  4 is the control that matters. At 2.6 epochs (exactly\nthe exposure at which Crema-D produced + 0.297 +0.297 ) the mined\ncorpus produces − 0.0007 -0.0007 . Four times the data, captions that explicitly\nname the target attribute, an identical recipe, matched optimization, and\nno effect. \n\n Keyword accuracy meanwhile rises to 0.929. The mined set is\nlargely human vocalization, so it functions as further vocal-content\nsupervision. This is consistent with the same competition that produced\nthe original regression in Section  4 : the corpus pushes on the\naxis the model already favors (Figure  1 ). \n\n 7 The deficit is not truncation \n\n Evaluating at full dimensionality recovers only 18% of the emotion gap,\nso the deficit is not information hidden in the truncated rungs. Because\nthe embedding is Matryoshka-nested, a deficit measured at the default\nrung could in principle reflect information that is present but\ntruncated. Table  5 evaluates Ravdess at the default and at\nfull dimensionality for both the pre- and post-speech models. \n\n Ravdess \n d = 1024 d{=}1024 \n d = 2048 d{=}2048 \n\n pre-speech \n 0.348 \n 0.337 \n\n post-speech \n 0.211 \n 0.224 \n\n gap \n 0.137 \n 0.113 \n\n Table 5: Full dimensionality recovers about 18% of the gap (0.137 to\n0.113) and slightly lowers the pre-speech model. The prosody\ninformation is not present-but-truncated. \n\n Doubling the read-out closes 18% of the gap and reduces the pre-speech\nmodel’s own score, which is not the signature of information waiting in\nthe upper rungs. \n\n 8 The intervention is otherwise inert \n\n The mined fine-tune leaves a nine-task board essentially unchanged, so\nits null effect on emotion is not masked by broad damage. The remaining\nalternative was that the fine-tune degrades the representation\ngenerally; Table  6 rules this out. \n\n task \n base \n mined \n Δ \\Delta \n\n SpeechCommands \n 0.894 \n 0.929 \n + 0.035 +0.035 \n\n MACS T2A \n 0.137 \n 0.150 \n + 0.013 +0.013 \n\n GTZAN reranking \n 0.717 \n 0.721 \n + 0.005 +0.005 \n\n Clotho T2A \n 0.300 \n 0.301 \n + 0.002 +0.002 \n\n VehicleSound clust. \n 0.025 \n 0.025 \n + 0.001 +0.001 \n\n Ravdess \n 0.211 \n 0.210 \n − 0.001 -0.001 \n\n UrbanSound8K T2A \n 0.010 \n 0.009 \n − 0.001 -0.001 \n\n BeijingOpera \n 0.924 \n 0.919 \n − 0.004 -0.004 \n\n GTZAN genre \n 0.644 \n 0.635 \n − 0.009 -0.009 \n\n Table 6: The mined fine-tune is not broadly destructive. Across a\nnine-task board, no cell is degraded by more than 0.009 0.009 ; the only\ngains above that are keyword spotting ( + 0.035 +0.035 ) and MACS text-to-audio\nretrieval ( + 0.013 +0.013 ). Rows are ordered by Δ \\Delta . \n\n The mined corpus is therefore not harming the representation. It simply\ndoes not teach prosody. \n\n Figure 3: An attribute is encoded only when the negatives cannot be\nseparated without it. Left: in the prosody-controlled corpus, lexical\ncontent is held fixed, so prosody is the only remaining signal and the\nobjective cannot be minimized without encoding it. Right: in mined\nnatural captions, scenes differ, so items are separable by scene content\nalone; emotion is named in the caption but never required . \n\n 9 Caption separability predicts what is learned \n\n A text-side statistic, computed from the corpus alone with no training,\norders the corpora exactly as their outcomes do. The account above\nimplies such a property: if the text side alone resolves an anchor from\nits negatives, the audio side is never forced to carry the attribute. We\ntherefore measure, directly from the cached text embeddings, how\nconfusable an item’s caption is with the captions of its negatives. \n\n For sampled negative sets of 128 items we report two statistics\n(Table  7 ): collision rate , the fraction of anchors\nwhose exact caption also appears on another item in the set; and\n mean max similarity , the average cosine similarity between an\nanchor’s caption and its most similar distractor. Both are properties of\nthe corpus, not of any trained model. \n\n corpus \n clips/cap. \n coll. \n max sim \n\n Crema-D \n 19.90 \n 0.556 \n 0.971 \n\n mined emotion \n 1.16 \n 0.038 \n 0.827 \n\n AudioCaps \n 1.09 \n 0.003 \n 0.804 \n\n spoken words \n 5.53 \n 0.006 \n 0.759 \n\n Table 7: Text-side separability singles out the one corpus that taught\nprosody. In Crema-D , 55.6% of anchors have an exact caption duplicate\namong their negatives and the nearest distractor sits at cosine 0.971:\nthe text side cannot resolve the positive. Every other corpus resolves\neasily. \n\n Crema-D , the only corpus that taught prosody, is an outlier by more\nthan an order of magnitude in collision rate. The mined corpus, whose\ncaptions all name emotion, is 14 × \\times less confusable and did not\nteach it. The spoken-word corpus that produced the original regression is\nthe least confusable of all: single-word captions are semantically\nfar apart, so the text side resolves everything and the audio side need\nonly encode which word was said. \n\n This inverts a standard quality heuristic. Caption diversity is normally\na virtue, and by that measure Crema-D is the worst corpus in the table.\nIt is the only one that works, because low diversity is what forces the\naudio side to do the discriminating. We suggest reporting these\nstatistics alongside corpus size when a contrastive corpus is introduced,\nsince they are free to compute and predict which attributes the corpus\ncan teach. \n\n 10 Mechanism \n\n We propose that a contrastive objective encodes an attribute only when\nits negative set cannot be separated without it (Figure  3 ). \n\n The negative set is not the batch. \n\n Our objective draws\nnegatives from the batch together with a FIFO memory bank of\nfrozen text embeddings, so the relevant comparison set is far larger than\na single batch and is drawn from the corpus at large. This matters for\nthe argument, and it sharpens rather than weakens it: the question is not\nwhether 128 sampled items happen to be confusable, but whether the\n corpus supplies text-side negatives that make the attribute\nnecessary. A corpus with few distinct captions is confusable everywhere,\nnot just occasionally. \n\n In Crema-D , sentence content is fixed by construction, and the corpus\ncontains only 374 distinct captions across 7,442 clips. Any negative\ndrawn from the bank is therefore likely to carry near-identical text to\nthe anchor. The text side cannot resolve the positive, so the loss is not\nminimizable without representing prosody. The attribute is\n required . \n\n In the mined corpus, an item such as “a man yelling and ducks in the\nbackground” does name the emotional attribute, but its 25,392 distinct\ncaptions mean almost every negative differs in scene content. The item is\nseparable by scene alone (ducks, vehicles, water), so the loss\nreaches its minimum without ever encoding how the voice sounds. The\nattribute is mentioned but not required . \n\n Note that this makes caption diversity, ordinarily a marker of corpus\nquality, the very property that lets the model skip the attribute.\nSection  9 makes that quantitative. \n\n This account explains the failure of scale in Section  6 , the\nsuccess of a much smaller corpus in Section  5 , and the\noriginal regression in Section  4 : a corpus of isolated spoken\nwords makes lexical identity the discriminative axis, so prosody is not\nmerely unsupervised but selected against. \n\n A testable prediction. \n\n If the mechanism is correct, the mined\ncorpus should encode emotion once emotion is made the residual\ndiscriminative axis, with no new audio. Section  11 tests this\ndirectly, first under the most literal reading (regroup the batches) and\nthen under the sharper one the mechanism actually implies (remove the\ntext-side shortcut so the negatives cannot be separated without emotion). \n\n 11 Testing the mechanism \n\n Restructuring the negatives of the mined corpus recovers emotion with no\nnew audio, exactly as the mechanism predicts: emotion returns when\nprosody becomes the only way to tell training items apart, and not\nbefore. We test this on the\n identical mined clips, holding the fine-tuning recipe of\nSection  3 fixed and changing only the structure of the\ncontrastive negatives. Both interventions initialize from the post-speech\nmodel and run to the same 2.6-epoch exposure as Section  6 .\nFigure  4 summarizes the outcome. \n\n Figure 4: Caption collision, not caption similarity, recovers emotion.\nThe same mined audio is trained under four corpus structures ( Ravdess \nzero-shot emotion): grouping batches by scene similarity (E6a) does not\nmove emotion, while collapsing caption diversity so that emotion is the\nrequired discriminative axis (E6b) recovers it most of the way to the\npre-speech level (dashed), with none of the audio changed. Crema-D , a\ndifferent, prosody-controlled audio corpus, is shown for reference. \n\n 11.1 Similarity is not enough \n\n Scene-grouped batching raises caption similarity and leaves emotion\nunmoved. The most literal reading of the mechanism is to group each batch by scene. We order the\ncorpus so that every batch of 128 is a nearest-neighbor cluster in the\nspace of emotion-stripped caption embeddings, which raises the mean\nwithin-batch caption similarity from 0.65 0.65 to 0.71 0.71 (nearest-distractor\ncosine 0.83 → 0.89 0.83\\to 0.89 ) while keeping 6.6 6.6 emotions per batch on average,\nand we draw negatives from the batch alone. Table  8 shows the\nresult: scene-grouped batching does not recover emotion ( Ravdess 0.222 0.222 ,\nagainst a random-batch control of 0.231 0.231 and a pre-intervention baseline\nof 0.216 0.216 ). \n\n in-batch negatives \n Ravdess \n SpeechCommands \n\n post-speech base \n 0.216 \n 0.915 \n\n random control \n 0.231 \n 0.926 \n\n scene-grouped \n 0.222 \n 0.931 \n\n Table 8: Scene-grouped batching (E6a) leaves emotion unmoved despite\nraising the nearest-distractor caption cosine to 0.89 0.89 (within-batch\nmean similarity 0.65 → 0.71 0.65\\to 0.71 ). Grouping raised similarity but not\n collision : captions stayed distinct (exact-duplicate rate 0.04 0.04 ,\nversus Crema-D ’s 0.56 0.56 ), so the text side could still resolve items by\nfine caption differences and prosody was never required. \n\n This sharpens the mechanism. The operative property is not proximity but\n collision : Crema-D works because many items carry the same\ncaption, not because their captions are merely similar. Grouping moved the\nsimilarity statistic of Section  9 without moving the collision\nstatistic, and emotion did not move with it. \n\n 11.2 Collision recovers emotion \n\n Collapsing caption diversity so that emotion is the only separating axis\nrecovers it on the same audio (Table  9 ). To remove the\ntext-side shortcut directly, we relabel every mined clip with a Crema-D -style emotion-tone caption (for example\n“Speech with an angry tone, spoken by a person”), assigning the label\nfrom the clip’s dominant emotion keyword, and cache these as the text\ntargets; the audio is untouched. This gives the corpus Crema-D ’s\nstructure: caption diversity collapses from 25 , 392 25{,}392 distinct captions\nto 16 16 , the within-batch collision rate rises from 0.04 0.04 to 0.98 0.98 , and\nthe full-corpus frozen-text bank now makes emotion the only axis that\nseparates an anchor from its many same-caption negatives. The control is\nthe identical run on the original scene-descriptive captions. \n\n mined captions \n Ravdess \n SpeechCommands \n\n scene-descriptive \n 0.221 \n 0.930 \n\n emotion-tone \n 0.310 \n 0.903 \n\n Crema-D audio (ref) \n 0.508 \n 0.845 \n\n Table 9: Caption collision (E6b): coarsening the mined captions from\n 25 , 392 25{,}392 distinct to 16 16 Crema-D -style emotion-tone labels makes\nemotion the required axis on the same mined audio, recovering\n Ravdess by + 0.089 +0.089 , most of the way back to the pre-speech level\n( 0.348 0.348 ), while keyword accuracy trades back. Three seeds each, random\nbatching with the full-corpus text bank: control 0.221 0.221 \n( 0.217 / 0.224 / 0.222 0.217/0.224/0.222 ), collision 0.310 0.310 ( 0.308 / 0.308 / 0.315 0.308/0.308/0.315 ), positive\nat every seed with non-overlapping ranges. Crema-D audio ( 374 374 \ncaptions) is shown for reference; only the caption structure differs\nbetween the two mined rows. \n\n The same clips that produced no effect as scene descriptions\n(Section  6 ) recover emotion once relabeled so that emotion is\nrequired (Table  9 ). The effect is large ( + 0.089 +0.089 Ravdess ) and holds at every seed\nwith non-overlapping ranges, and it is not free: keyword accuracy falls\nfrom 0.930 0.930 to 0.903 0.903 , the same competition that produced the original\nregression in Section  4 , now running in reverse. The recovery\nis partial ( 0.310 0.310 against Crema-D ’s 0.508 0.508 on the same eval),\nconsistent with the mined vocalization clips carrying a noisier prosodic\nsignal than Crema-D ’s controlled recordings. \n\n A second, non-acted benchmark. \n\n On Iemocap (Busso et al., 2008 ) emotion\nclassification (a different corpus, improvised rather than lexically\nmatched), the same recovery appears in sign but attenuated: the collision\nmodel scores 0.223 0.223 against the scene-caption control’s 0.204 0.204 and the\nbaseline’s 0.201 0.201 (three-seed means; the gap is positive at every seed\nwith non-overlapping ranges, but roughly a quarter of the Ravdess effect).\nThe main effect is therefore strong within the acted-emotion paradigm and\ndirectionally, seed-robustly confirmed outside it, not uniform across\nemotion corpora. \n\n 12 Discussion \n\n The practical reading is that “add data containing the attribute” is\nnot a well-formed instruction for contrastive training. The mined corpus\nsatisfies that instruction completely (every clip’s caption names\nvocal emotion), and it does nothing. The controlled corpus violates the\nusual desiderata for corpus quality, in that it has only 374 distinct\ncaptions across 7,442 clips and therefore a high false-negative rate,\nand it works. \n\n This inverts the usual quality heuristics. Caption diversity, corpus\nsize, and label coverage are all proxies for the thing that matters, and\nin this case they are anti-correlated with it. What matters is whether\nthe corpus makes the target attribute necessary for discrimination. \n\n A corollary worth stating: this predicts that mixing a controlled corpus\ninto a large natural one may dilute exactly the property that makes it\nwork, since the controlled items would be batched against natural\nnegatives and become separable without prosody. If so, the intervention\nbelongs at the batch level rather than the corpus level. We have not\ntested this. \n\n 13 Limitations \n\n The fine-tuning arms of Sections  4 through  8 are\nsingle runs; only the interventions of Section  11 are\nreplicated across three seeds. The single-run effect sizes are large\nrelative to typical seed variance in this setup, but we do not report\nseed replicates for them, and the quantitative exchange rates in\nSection  5 should be read as approximate rather than measured. \n\n The emotion axis rests principally on a single benchmark, and the two\ncorpora share\nmore structure than is ideal for a clean test. Crema-D and Ravdess are\nboth acted corpora with categorical labels, and both hold lexical\ncontent approximately fixed across emotions: Livingstone and Russo ( 2018 ) \nuse lexically-matched statements by design, exactly the property we argue\nmakes Crema-D effective. Section  5 is therefore partly\ndomain transfer, and the recovery may be assisted by the evaluation\nsharing the training corpus’s structural bias. We therefore report a\nsecond, non-acted emotion benchmark, Iemocap , on which the\nstructural recovery of Section  11 replicates in sign but at a\nmuch smaller magnitude.\nThe main effect should be read as strong within the acted-emotion\nparadigm and directionally confirmed outside it, not as uniform across\nemotion corpora; the mined vocalization corpus transfers imperfectly to\nconversational emotion. \n\n The keyword gains in Section  4 follow from training on\nspoken-word data. This is a genuine capability gain but it is not\nzero-shot generalization in the strict sense for this model, and we do\nnot claim it as such. \n\n The mechanism is no longer only an inference from the contrast between\ntwo corpora. Section  11 tests it directly on the same audio:\nraising in-batch caption similarity leaves emotion unmoved, while\ncollapsing caption diversity so that emotion is the required\ndiscriminative axis recovers it. We are deliberate about what this does\nand does not show. The emotion-tone relabeling is itself a supervised\nsignal, and a reader could call it a relabeling into a classification\ntask. The point is that the same signal, present as free text in\nthe mined captions of Section  6 , produced no effect, and\nbecame usable only once the corpus structure made it necessary. What the\nintervention isolates is that structure, not the mere presence of the\nlabel, is the operative variable. It remains a single base model and a\nsingle audio pipeline; whether the effect is objective-level rather than\narchitecture-specific rests on the model-agnostic prior results we cite\nin Section  2 . \n\n Data contamination. \n\n Our pretraining corpus includes 70K\nCommon Voice clips sampled from the validated pool. In Common\nVoice, validated is the superset from which train, dev, and test\nsplits are drawn, and the corpus is cumulative across releases. Replaying\nour (deterministic) ingest to recover the exact sampled clip identifiers,\nwe measure 44 of 200 (22%) of the CommonVoice v21 mini test\nclips present in our training set , alongside 47% verbatim sentence\noverlap. The cause is structural rather than accidental: the ingest\nstreams the archive in order and stops at a target count, archive order\ntracks clip identifier, and low identifiers are the older recordings that\nthe v21 test split draws from, so “the first N N that pass the filters”\nsystematically over-samples the evaluation era. The effect is compounded\nbecause our spoken-word corpus is itself derived from Common Voice. We\ntherefore do not report any Common Voice retrieval result for these\nmodels, and the evaluations in this paper are drawn from tasks whose\nsource corpora we do not train on. \n\n We flag the general hazard, since it is not specific to us: several\ncorpora in wide use as training data (Common Voice, Crema-D ,\nIEMOCAP, VoxPopuli, FLEURS, GigaSpeech) are simultaneously tasks \nin audio embedding benchmarks. A model trained on the obvious speech data\nis contaminated on several cells by default, and sampling from a pooled\n“validated” split rather than a designated train split makes this\nlikely rather than possible. Corpora intended for training should record\nsource clip identifiers so that overlap can be audited after the fact;\nours did not, and recovering them required replaying the ingest. \n\n Finally, Crema-D is distributed under a share-alike database license\nand is used here for controlled experimentation only. No model weights\ntrained on it are released, and no model trained on it is submitted to a\nbenchmark whose tasks derive from it. \n\n 14 Conclusion \n\n Adding a large lexical-speech round to a contrastive audio embedding\nimproved keyword spotting by 76 points and cost 14 points of emotion\nrecognition. The lost attribute was recovered by a corpus 120 × 120\\times \nsmaller than the training corpus, whose construction made prosody the\nonly way to tell training items apart, and was not recovered at all by a\ncorpus four times larger\nwhose captions named emotion explicitly but whose items remained\nseparable without it. A direct intervention closes the loop: restructuring\nthat same mined corpus so that emotion is the only axis separating\ntraining items recovers it, while raising in-batch similarity alone does\nnot, so the structural property is a cause and not a correlate. What a\ncontrastive corpus teaches is decided by what its negatives make\nnecessary. Data volume and caption vocabulary are poor proxies for that,\nand optimizing them is not a substitute for designing it. To give a\ncontrastive embedding an attribute, construct the corpus so that its\nnegatives cannot be separated without that attribute. \n\n References \n\n Bleeker et al. (2024) \n\nMaurits Bleeker, Mariya Hendriksen, Andrew Yates, and Maarten de Rijke. 2024.\n\n Demonstrating and reducing shortcuts in vision-language representation learning.\n\n In Transactions on Machine Learning Research (TMLR) .\n\n ArXiv:2402.17510.\n\n Busso et al. (2008) \n\nCarlos Busso, Murtaza Bulut, Chi-Chun Lee, Abe Kazemzadeh, Emily Mower, Samuel Kim, Jeannette N. Chang, Sungbok Lee, and Shrikanth S. Narayanan. 2008.\n\n IEMOCAP: Interactive emotional dyadic motion capture database.\n\n Language Resources and Evaluation , 42(4):335–359.\n\n Cao et al. (2014) \n\nHouwei Cao, David G. Cooper, Michael K. Keutmann, Ruben C. Gur, Ani Nenkova, and Ragini Verma. 2014.\n\n CREMA-D: Crowd-sourced emotional multimodal actors dataset .\n\n IEEE Transactions on Affective Computing , 5(4):377–390.\n\n Chen et al. (2021) \n\nTing Chen, Calvin Luo, and Lala Li. 2021.\n\n Intriguing properties of contrastive losses.\n\n In Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS) .\n\n ArXiv:2011.02803.\n\n Elizalde et al. (2023) \n\nBenjamin Elizalde, Soham Deshmukh, Mahmoud Al Ismail, and Huaming Wang. 2023.\n\n CLAP: Learning audio concepts from natural language supervision.\n\n In IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP) .\n\n ArXiv:2206.04769.\n\n Geirhos et al. (2020) \n\nRobert Geirhos, Jörn-Henrik Jacobsen, Claudio Michaelis, Richard Zemel, Wieland Brendel, Matthias Bethge, and Felix A. Wichmann. 2020.\n\n Shortcut learning in deep neural networks.\n\n arXiv preprint arXiv:2004.07780 .\n\n Kusupati et al. (2022) \n\nAditya Kusupati, Gantavya Bhatt, Aniket Rege, Matthew Wallingford, Aditya Sinha, Vivek Ramanujan, William Howard-Snyder, Kaifeng Chen, Sham Kakade, Prateek Jain, and Ali Farhadi. 2022.\n\n Matryoshka representation learning.\n\n arXiv preprint arXiv:2205.13147 .\n\n Livingstone and Russo (2018) \n\nSteven R. Livingstone and Frank A. Russo. 2018.\n\n The Ryerson audio-visual database of emotional speech and song (RAVDESS): A dynamic, multimodal set of facial and vocal expressions in North American English .\n\n PLoS ONE , 13(5):e0196391.\n\n Martel et al. (2026) \n\nHéctor Martel, Joe Hennessy-Priest, and Taemin Cho. 2026.\n\n Probing low-level acoustic attribute encoding in CLAP audio embeddings.\n\n Proc. International Conference on Digital Audio Effects (DAFx) .\n\n ArXiv:2607.03806.\n\n Mei et al. (2023) \n\nXinhao Mei, Chutong Meng, Haohe Liu, Qiuqiang Kong, Tom Ko, Chengqi Zhao, Mark D. Plumbley, and Yuexian Zou. 2023.\n\n WavCaps: A ChatGPT-assisted weakly-labelled audio captioning dataset for audio-language multimodal research.\n\n arXiv preprint arXiv:2303.17395 .\n\n Muennighoff et al. (2022) \n\nNiklas Muennighoff, Nouamane Tazi, Loïc Magne, and Nils Reimers. 2022.\n\n MTEB: Massive text embedding benchmark.\n\n arXiv preprint arXiv:2210.07316 .\n\n Pan et al. (2023) \n\nYu Pan, Yanni Hu, Yuguang Yang, Wen Fei, Jixun Yao, Heng Lu, Lei Ma, and Jianjun Zhao. 2023.\n\n GEmo-CLAP: Gender-attribute-enhanced contrastive language-audio pretraining for accurate speech emotion recognition.\n\n arXiv preprint arXiv:2306.07848 .\n\n Robinson et al. (2020) \n\nJoshua Robinson, Ching-Yao Chuang, Suvrit Sra, and Stefanie Jegelka. 2020.\n\n Contrastive learning with hard negative samples.\n\n arXiv preprint arXiv:2010.04592 .\n\n Robinson et al. (2021) \n\nJoshua Robinson, Li Sun, Ke Yu, Kayhan Batmanghelich, Stefanie Jegelka, and Suvrit Sra. 2021.\n\n Can contrastive learning avoid shortcut solutions?\n\n arXiv preprint arXiv:2106.11230 .\n\n Tonmoy et al. (2026) \n\nAbdul Basit Tonmoy, Kazi Fardinul Hoque, Md. Shahrier Islam Arham, and Arman Luthra. 2026.\n\n Fusion embedding: A unified embedding space for text,