{"type": "assistant", "message": {"id": "msg_1785823163611", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "我已经掌握了所有需要的事实锚点。基础模型 (fusion-embedding) 拥有开源权重 (GitHub + HuggingFace)。让我再确认一下这篇论文本身是否发布了代码或用于微调的脚本。"}], "model": "GLM-5.2-w4a8", "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "7959668d-df83-4761-87bb-619bb93714f6", "uuid": "e407106b-d669-4793-ba92-5046cf007711"}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"id": "msg_1785823163611", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "tool_use", "id": "chatcmpl-tool-821bef33e3d960b4", "name": "Bash", "input": {"command": "curl -sL --max-time 15 \"https://github.com/Eximius-Labs/fusion-embedding\" 2>&1 | python3 -c \"\nimport sys, re\nhtml = sys.stdin.read()\n# Look for any mention of this paper, emotion, prosody, Crema-D, or fine-tuning scripts\nfor kw in ['2608.01560', 'emotion', 'prosody', 'Crema-D', 'crema', 'discriminative', 'corpus structure', 'fine-tune', 'finetune']:\n    if kw.lower() in html.lower():\n        print(f'FOUND: {kw}')\n# Check README content\nm = re.search(r'<article[^>]*>(.*?)</article>', html, re.DOTALL)\nif m:\n    text = re.sub(r'<[^>]+>', ' ', m.group(1))\n    text = re.sub(r'\\s+', ' ', text).strip()\n    print('README_PREVIEW:', text[:2000])\n\" 2>&1 | head -40", "description": "Check if base model repo mentions this paper or fine-tuning scripts"}}], "model": "GLM-5.2-w4a8", "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "7959668d-df83-4761-87bb-619bb93714f6", "uuid": "e8dd841f-1e04-4344-a9df-0484ea4bb0f3"}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-821bef33e3d960b4", "type": "tool_result", "content": "FOUND: 2608.01560\nFOUND: emotion\nFOUND: fine-tune\nREADME_PREVIEW: One model. One vector space. Text, image, video, audio, and the senses a robot actually has. An open-weight multimodal embedding model that extends a state-of-the-art vision-language base with new senses, without modifying a single base weight. What is Fusion Embedding? Fusion Embedding is a family of open-weight embedding models that map seven modalities into a single shared vector space : text, image, video, audio, thermal, inertial motion, and touch. It is built for retrieval, RAG, clustering and cross-modal search, and it is fully self-hostable . The reason it spans that many is architectural. The base model is frozen and each new sense arrives as a separately loadable pack, so text, image and video vectors stay bit-for-bit identical to the base and gaining a modality never costs a re-index. In practice a sensor nobody has modeled before is a small trained head and an afternoon on one GPU. Two architecture generations are released: fusion-embedding-1 (connector-only, final at v0.3) and fusion-embedding-2 (connector + modality-gated deep adapters — the current line). Instead of training a multimodal embedder from scratch, Fusion takes Qwen3-VL-Embedding — an open state-of-the-art text/image/video embedding model — freezes it byte-for-byte , and adds an audio pathway to it: a frozen Qwen2.5-Omni audio tower feeds a small trained connector (the FusionResampler , ~16M parameters, &lt;1% of the base) that translates audio into the base's input space. Audio is aligned to text contrastively; because text, image, and video already share the base's space, audio↔image and audio↔video alignment emerge through the text bridge (the ImageBind property). Generation 2 adds modality-gated deep adapters (44.2M parameters across the base's 28 layers) that give the frozen LM in-layer capacity to process audio — active only on audio inputs, exact pass-throughs for everything else (see fusion-embedding-2 ). The result: the base model's text, image, and video embeddings are provably u", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "7959668d-df83-4761-87bb-619bb93714f6", "uuid": "82759452-4a12-40aa-9b88-bd5e5a0fdfb7", "timestamp": "2026-08-04T06:00:17.067Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "FOUND: 2608.01560\nFOUND: emotion\nFOUND: fine-tune\nREADME_PREVIEW: One model. One vector space. Text, image, video, audio, and the senses a robot actually has. An open-weight multimodal embedding model that extends a state-of-the-art vision-language base with new senses, without modifying a single base weight. What is Fusion Embedding? Fusion Embedding is a family of open-weight embedding models that map seven modalities into a single shared vector space : text, image, video, audio, thermal, inertial motion, and touch. It is built for retrieval, RAG, clustering and cross-modal search, and it is fully self-hostable . The reason it spans that many is architectural. The base model is frozen and each new sense arrives as a separately loadable pack, so text, image and video vectors stay bit-for-bit identical to the base and gaining a modality never costs a re-index. In practice a sensor nobody has modeled before is a small trained head and an afternoon on one GPU. Two architecture generations are released: fusion-embedding-1 (connector-only, final at v0.3) and fusion-embedding-2 (connector + modality-gated deep adapters — the current line). Instead of training a multimodal embedder from scratch, Fusion takes Qwen3-VL-Embedding — an open state-of-the-art text/image/video embedding model — freezes it byte-for-byte , and adds an audio pathway to it: a frozen Qwen2.5-Omni audio tower feeds a small trained connector (the FusionResampler , ~16M parameters, &lt;1% of the base) that translates audio into the base's input space. Audio is aligned to text contrastively; because text, image, and video already share the base's space, audio↔image and audio↔video alignment emerge through the text bridge (the ImageBind property). Generation 2 adds modality-gated deep adapters (44.2M parameters across the base's 28 layers) that give the frozen LM in-layer capacity to process audio — active only on audio inputs, exact pass-throughs for everything else (see fusion-embedding-2 ). The result: the base model's text, image, and video embeddings are provably u", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
